1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,811
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,991 --> 00:00:04,058
Most people have tried to budget at

3
00:00:04,058 --> 00:00:04,707
least once.

4
00:00:04,887 --> 00:00:06,263
They download an app,

5
00:00:06,263 --> 00:00:07,508
make a spreadsheet,

6
00:00:07,508 --> 00:00:08,687
set strict limits,

7
00:00:08,687 --> 00:00:10,128
and three weeks later,

8
00:00:10,128 --> 00:00:11,110
it's abandoned.

9
00:00:11,290 --> 00:00:12,089
If that's you,

10
00:00:12,089 --> 00:00:13,914
the problem probably wasn't you.

11
00:00:14,094 --> 00:00:15,139
It was the system.

12
00:00:15,319 --> 00:00:17,583
Today, I'll show you a complete budget

13
00:00:17,583 --> 00:00:19,609
system built to survive real life,

14
00:00:19,609 --> 00:00:21,635
and why it works when others fail.

15
00:00:21,984 --> 00:00:24,365
Budgets fail for predictable reasons.

16
00:00:24,545 --> 00:00:25,777
They're too strict,

17
00:00:25,777 --> 00:00:28,631
too complicated, or they depend on willpower

18
00:00:28,631 --> 00:00:29,734
every single day.

19
00:00:29,914 --> 00:00:31,655
They ignore irregular bills,

20
00:00:31,655 --> 00:00:33,768
so the first car repair blows them

21
00:00:33,768 --> 00:00:33,954
up.

22
00:00:34,134 --> 00:00:36,349
And they make people feel guilty,

23
00:00:36,349 --> 00:00:39,034
which is a terrible long term motivator.

24
00:00:39,383 --> 00:00:41,708
A budget that actually works does the

25
00:00:41,708 --> 00:00:42,274
opposite.

26
00:00:42,454 --> 00:00:43,209
It's simple.

27
00:00:43,389 --> 00:00:45,362
It runs mostly on autopilot.

28
00:00:45,542 --> 00:00:47,969
It plans for irregular costs.

29
00:00:48,149 --> 00:00:50,220
And it includes real money for fun,

30
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:51,818
so you don't feel deprived.

31
00:00:51,998 --> 00:00:54,001
The goal isn't to track every penny

32
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:54,459
forever.

33
00:00:54,639 --> 00:00:56,777
It's to make good money decisions the

34
00:00:56,777 --> 00:00:57,240
default.

35
00:00:57,589 --> 00:00:59,621
We'll look at this from three angles.

36
00:00:59,801 --> 00:01:00,967
First, the numbers:

37
00:01:00,967 --> 00:01:04,036
where the average household's money actually goes,

38
00:01:04,036 --> 00:01:06,430
and how popular budget rules divide it.

39
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:08,312
Second, the psychology:

40
00:01:08,312 --> 00:01:10,457
why our brains fight budgets,

41
00:01:10,457 --> 00:01:12,972
and how to work with them instead.

42
00:01:13,152 --> 00:01:14,639
And third, the playbook:

43
00:01:14,639 --> 00:01:16,620
a twelve step system you can set

44
00:01:16,620 --> 00:01:17,611
up this weekend.

45
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:20,290
Stay for the sample budgets near the

46
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:20,549
end.

47
00:01:20,729 --> 00:01:23,002
I'll show the system at three different

48
00:01:23,002 --> 00:01:23,817
income levels.

49
00:01:23,997 --> 00:01:25,600
Let's start with the numbers.

50
00:01:25,949 --> 00:01:28,960
Household spending data comes from the Bureau

51
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:32,172
of Labor Statistics Consumer Expenditure Survey.

52
00:01:32,352 --> 00:01:35,127
The fifty thirty twenty rule comes from

53
00:01:35,127 --> 00:01:37,689
the book All Your Worth by Elizabeth

54
00:01:37,689 --> 00:01:39,895
Warren and Amelia Warren Tyagi,

55
00:01:39,895 --> 00:01:41,176
published in 2005.

56
00:01:41,356 --> 00:01:45,317
Subscription spending comes from a 2022 survey

57
00:01:45,317 --> 00:01:47,126
by C plus R Research.

58
00:01:47,306 --> 00:01:50,411
Behavioral research comes from published studies,

59
00:01:50,411 --> 00:01:51,869
which we name as we go.

60
00:01:52,049 --> 00:01:53,361
This is education,

61
00:01:53,361 --> 00:01:54,893
not financial advice.

62
00:01:55,243 --> 00:01:55,963
Angle one.

63
00:01:56,143 --> 00:01:56,898
The numbers.

64
00:01:57,078 --> 00:01:58,773
Where does the money actually go?

65
00:01:59,122 --> 00:02:02,657
According to the BLS Consumer Expenditure Survey,

66
00:02:02,657 --> 00:02:06,336
the average American household spent around seventy

67
00:02:06,336 --> 00:02:08,573
seven thousand dollars in 2023.

68
00:02:08,753 --> 00:02:11,493
Housing was the biggest category by far,

69
00:02:11,493 --> 00:02:13,617
around a third of all spending.

70
00:02:13,797 --> 00:02:15,672
Transportation came next,

71
00:02:15,672 --> 00:02:17,547
around seventeen percent.

72
00:02:17,727 --> 00:02:20,084
Food was around thirteen percent.

73
00:02:20,434 --> 00:02:24,046
Together, housing, transportation and food make up

74
00:02:24,046 --> 00:02:26,430
around six of every ten dollars a

75
00:02:26,430 --> 00:02:28,236
typical household spends.

76
00:02:28,416 --> 00:02:29,796
Then come healthcare,

77
00:02:29,796 --> 00:02:31,898
personal insurance and pensions,

78
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:34,198
entertainment, and everything else.

79
00:02:34,378 --> 00:02:36,828
That's why the biggest budget wins almost

80
00:02:36,828 --> 00:02:38,681
always come from the big three,

81
00:02:38,681 --> 00:02:39,637
not from coffee.

82
00:02:39,987 --> 00:02:43,268
Housing, transportation and food make up around

83
00:02:43,268 --> 00:02:45,780
six of every ten dollars the average

84
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:45,920
U.

85
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:46,390
S.

86
00:02:46,570 --> 00:02:49,671
household spends, according to the BLS Consumer

87
00:02:49,671 --> 00:02:50,924
Expenditure Survey.

88
00:02:51,274 --> 00:02:53,387
Now let's look at a popular framework.

89
00:02:53,567 --> 00:02:56,115
In their 2005 book All Your Worth,

90
00:02:56,115 --> 00:02:59,636
Elizabeth Warren and her daughter Amelia Warren

91
00:02:59,636 --> 00:03:03,082
Tyagi suggested dividing after tax income into

92
00:03:03,082 --> 00:03:03,981
three parts.

93
00:03:04,161 --> 00:03:05,798
Fifty percent for needs.

94
00:03:05,978 --> 00:03:07,534
Thirty percent for wants.

95
00:03:07,714 --> 00:03:10,346
And twenty percent for savings and debt

96
00:03:10,346 --> 00:03:10,953
payments.

97
00:03:11,133 --> 00:03:13,139
It became known as the fifty thirty

98
00:03:13,139 --> 00:03:13,827
twenty rule.

99
00:03:14,177 --> 00:03:15,998
On a take home pay of five

100
00:03:15,998 --> 00:03:17,748
thousand dollars a month,

101
00:03:17,748 --> 00:03:20,339
that's twenty five hundred for needs,

102
00:03:20,339 --> 00:03:22,020
like housing, utilities,

103
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:25,311
groceries, insurance and minimum debt payments.

104
00:03:25,491 --> 00:03:27,520
Fifteen hundred for wants,

105
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:28,769
like dining out,

106
00:03:28,769 --> 00:03:30,251
hobbies and travel.

107
00:03:30,431 --> 00:03:32,494
And one thousand for savings,

108
00:03:32,494 --> 00:03:34,913
investing and extra debt payments.

109
00:03:35,262 --> 00:03:37,491
The fifty thirty twenty rule on five

110
00:03:37,491 --> 00:03:40,092
thousand dollars of monthly take home pay.

111
00:03:40,442 --> 00:03:42,126
It's a useful starting point.

112
00:03:42,306 --> 00:03:43,955
But in high cost cities,

113
00:03:43,955 --> 00:03:46,566
needs can easily exceed fifty percent.

114
00:03:46,746 --> 00:03:48,674
And if you want to retire early

115
00:03:48,674 --> 00:03:49,421
or catch up,

116
00:03:49,421 --> 00:03:51,721
twenty percent for savings may not be

117
00:03:51,721 --> 00:03:52,157
enough.

118
00:03:52,337 --> 00:03:54,525
So think of fifty thirty twenty as

119
00:03:54,525 --> 00:03:55,490
a starting map,

120
00:03:55,490 --> 00:03:56,133
not a law.

121
00:03:56,483 --> 00:03:58,109
Now the hidden leaks.

122
00:03:58,289 --> 00:04:00,318
In a 2022 survey by C plus

123
00:04:00,318 --> 00:04:03,909
R Research, people estimated they spent around

124
00:04:03,909 --> 00:04:07,344
eighty six dollars a month on subscriptions.

125
00:04:07,524 --> 00:04:09,537
When they actually added them up,

126
00:04:09,537 --> 00:04:12,098
the average was about two hundred nineteen

127
00:04:12,098 --> 00:04:12,586
dollars.

128
00:04:12,766 --> 00:04:14,278
That's more than two and a half

129
00:04:14,278 --> 00:04:15,448
times what they thought.

130
00:04:15,798 --> 00:04:17,905
Two hundred nineteen dollars a month is

131
00:04:17,905 --> 00:04:20,013
over twenty six hundred dollars a year.

132
00:04:20,193 --> 00:04:23,010
Invested instead at seven percent for twenty

133
00:04:23,010 --> 00:04:25,059
years, that could grow to over a

134
00:04:25,059 --> 00:04:26,659
hundred thousand dollars.

135
00:04:26,839 --> 00:04:28,860
Not every subscription is a waste.

136
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:30,722
But the ones you forgot about are

137
00:04:30,722 --> 00:04:31,385
pure leakage.

138
00:04:31,734 --> 00:04:34,660
And then there are irregular expenses:

139
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:36,738
car repairs, holiday gifts,

140
00:04:36,738 --> 00:04:38,740
annual insurance premiums,

141
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:40,357
back to school costs,

142
00:04:40,357 --> 00:04:41,127
vet bills.

143
00:04:41,307 --> 00:04:43,308
They don't show up every month,

144
00:04:43,308 --> 00:04:45,115
so most budgets ignore them.

145
00:04:45,295 --> 00:04:46,292
Then they arrive,

146
00:04:46,292 --> 00:04:47,582
and the budget breaks.

147
00:04:47,762 --> 00:04:48,950
In many households,

148
00:04:48,950 --> 00:04:51,137
these irregular costs can add up to

149
00:04:51,137 --> 00:04:53,137
several thousand dollars a year.

150
00:04:53,487 --> 00:04:55,450
Here's what a working budget can be

151
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:55,786
worth.

152
00:04:55,966 --> 00:04:58,254
Say the system helps you find three

153
00:04:58,254 --> 00:04:59,822
hundred dollars a month:

154
00:04:59,822 --> 00:05:01,391
some from subscriptions,

155
00:05:01,391 --> 00:05:02,371
some from food,

156
00:05:02,371 --> 00:05:04,790
some from a cheaper insurance policy.

157
00:05:04,970 --> 00:05:07,412
Invest that three hundred dollars a month

158
00:05:07,412 --> 00:05:09,796
at a seven percent average yearly return

159
00:05:09,796 --> 00:05:10,808
for thirty years,

160
00:05:10,808 --> 00:05:12,834
and it could grow to roughly three

161
00:05:12,834 --> 00:05:14,919
hundred sixty six thousand dollars.

162
00:05:15,269 --> 00:05:17,598
Three hundred dollars a month found in

163
00:05:17,598 --> 00:05:19,865
a budget, invested at seven percent a

164
00:05:19,865 --> 00:05:21,214
year for thirty years.

165
00:05:21,563 --> 00:05:23,328
That's the real reason to budget.

166
00:05:23,508 --> 00:05:24,913
Not to feel restricted,

167
00:05:24,913 --> 00:05:27,174
but to redirect money from things you

168
00:05:27,174 --> 00:05:29,130
don't care about to a future you

169
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:29,313
do.

170
00:05:29,493 --> 00:05:32,075
Three hundred dollars a month of forgotten

171
00:05:32,075 --> 00:05:34,903
spending, turned into a six figure investment.

172
00:05:35,083 --> 00:05:37,327
That's the power of giving every dollar

173
00:05:37,327 --> 00:05:37,672
a job.

174
00:05:38,022 --> 00:05:40,089
And the benefits aren't only financial.

175
00:05:40,269 --> 00:05:42,573
Many people report that having a clear

176
00:05:42,573 --> 00:05:45,120
plan reduces money stress and arguments at

177
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:45,424
home.

178
00:05:45,604 --> 00:05:47,688
When you know the bills are covered

179
00:05:47,688 --> 00:05:49,177
and the future is funded,

180
00:05:49,177 --> 00:05:52,036
everyday spending decisions become much lighter.

181
00:05:52,385 --> 00:05:54,788
So the numbers tell us three things.

182
00:05:54,968 --> 00:05:56,826
Focus on the big three.

183
00:05:57,006 --> 00:05:58,573
Watch for silent leaks.

184
00:05:58,753 --> 00:06:00,936
And plan for irregular costs.

185
00:06:01,116 --> 00:06:03,187
Now, why do our brains make this

186
00:06:03,187 --> 00:06:03,705
so hard?

187
00:06:03,885 --> 00:06:04,756
Angle two.

188
00:06:05,106 --> 00:06:06,070
Angle two.

189
00:06:06,250 --> 00:06:07,399
The psychology.

190
00:06:07,579 --> 00:06:10,060
Five reasons your brain fights your budget,

191
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:11,445
and how to work with it.

192
00:06:11,795 --> 00:06:12,643
Reason one.

193
00:06:12,823 --> 00:06:14,378
Restriction backfires.

194
00:06:14,558 --> 00:06:17,687
Research on dieting by psychologists Janet Polivy

195
00:06:17,687 --> 00:06:20,880
and Peter Herman described what's sometimes called

196
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,476
the what the hell effect.

197
00:06:22,656 --> 00:06:25,131
When strict dieters broke their rules,

198
00:06:25,131 --> 00:06:27,150
many gave up and ate even more.

199
00:06:27,330 --> 00:06:28,862
Budgets work the same way.

200
00:06:29,042 --> 00:06:29,836
Go over once,

201
00:06:29,836 --> 00:06:32,158
and it's tempting to abandon the whole

202
00:06:32,158 --> 00:06:32,525
thing.

203
00:06:32,875 --> 00:06:35,337
The fix is to build in flexibility.

204
00:06:35,517 --> 00:06:37,862
Include a real fun money category.

205
00:06:38,042 --> 00:06:40,085
Add a buffer for small surprises.

206
00:06:40,265 --> 00:06:41,724
And when you overspend,

207
00:06:41,724 --> 00:06:44,642
adjust next week instead of declaring failure.

208
00:06:44,822 --> 00:06:46,410
A budget is a plan,

209
00:06:46,410 --> 00:06:47,330
not a test.

210
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:48,644
Reason two.

211
00:06:48,824 --> 00:06:50,113
Decision fatigue.

212
00:06:50,293 --> 00:06:52,688
Every purchase becomes a small decision when

213
00:06:52,688 --> 00:06:54,484
you're trying to budget manually.

214
00:06:54,664 --> 00:06:55,593
Is this okay?

215
00:06:55,773 --> 00:06:56,841
Can I afford this?

216
00:06:57,021 --> 00:06:59,833
Making dozens of those choices every day

217
00:06:59,833 --> 00:07:00,817
is exhausting.

218
00:07:00,997 --> 00:07:04,561
Eventually, people stop deciding and just spend.

219
00:07:04,911 --> 00:07:07,628
The fix is automation and simple limits.

220
00:07:07,808 --> 00:07:11,319
When bills, savings and investing happen automatically

221
00:07:11,319 --> 00:07:13,659
on payday, the only decision left is

222
00:07:13,659 --> 00:07:15,935
how to spend a fixed weekly amount.

223
00:07:16,115 --> 00:07:18,112
One decision, not a hundred.

224
00:07:18,462 --> 00:07:19,786
Reason three.

225
00:07:19,966 --> 00:07:21,011
Mental accounting.

226
00:07:21,191 --> 00:07:22,798
Economist Richard Thaler,

227
00:07:22,798 --> 00:07:24,598
who later won a Nobel Prize,

228
00:07:24,598 --> 00:07:27,940
showed that people treat money differently depending

229
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:29,933
on which mental bucket it's in.

230
00:07:30,113 --> 00:07:32,656
A tax refund feels like free money.

231
00:07:32,836 --> 00:07:34,983
A bonus feels like fun money.

232
00:07:35,163 --> 00:07:36,742
But a dollar is a dollar.

233
00:07:37,092 --> 00:07:39,495
The fix is to use mental accounting

234
00:07:39,495 --> 00:07:40,250
on purpose.

235
00:07:40,430 --> 00:07:43,630
Create separate accounts for separate jobs:

236
00:07:43,630 --> 00:07:45,491
bills, spending, savings,

237
00:07:45,491 --> 00:07:46,235
investing.

238
00:07:46,415 --> 00:07:48,081
When the spending account is empty,

239
00:07:48,081 --> 00:07:49,271
you're done for the week.

240
00:07:49,451 --> 00:07:51,297
The buckets do the work for you.

241
00:07:51,647 --> 00:07:52,727
Reason four.

242
00:07:52,907 --> 00:07:53,807
Out of sight,

243
00:07:53,807 --> 00:07:54,637
out of mind.

244
00:07:54,817 --> 00:07:57,631
Card payments and phone wallets make spending

245
00:07:57,631 --> 00:07:58,695
almost invisible.

246
00:07:58,875 --> 00:08:01,540
Some studies suggest people may be willing

247
00:08:01,540 --> 00:08:03,635
to spend more when paying by card

248
00:08:03,635 --> 00:08:04,587
than with cash.

249
00:08:04,767 --> 00:08:06,713
When money doesn't feel real,

250
00:08:06,713 --> 00:08:08,122
it disappears faster.

251
00:08:08,472 --> 00:08:10,980
The fix is visibility without obsession.

252
00:08:11,160 --> 00:08:13,076
A weekly ten minute check of your

253
00:08:13,076 --> 00:08:14,643
spending account is enough.

254
00:08:14,823 --> 00:08:18,097
Some people use cash envelopes for categories

255
00:08:18,097 --> 00:08:19,406
they overspend in,

256
00:08:19,406 --> 00:08:20,570
like eating out.

257
00:08:20,750 --> 00:08:23,455
Seeing the balance shrink makes spending feel

258
00:08:23,455 --> 00:08:24,117
real again.

259
00:08:24,466 --> 00:08:25,732
And reason five.

260
00:08:25,912 --> 00:08:26,376
Shame.

261
00:08:26,556 --> 00:08:28,728
Many people avoid looking at their money

262
00:08:28,728 --> 00:08:30,411
because it makes them feel bad.

263
00:08:30,591 --> 00:08:32,530
But avoidance makes problems grow.

264
00:08:32,710 --> 00:08:34,610
The fix is to treat your budget

265
00:08:34,610 --> 00:08:36,204
like data, not a judgment.

266
00:08:36,384 --> 00:08:37,975
Numbers are information.

267
00:08:38,155 --> 00:08:39,873
They tell you what to adjust next.

268
00:08:40,223 --> 00:08:41,895
So what mindset works?

269
00:08:42,075 --> 00:08:44,211
Think of your budget as a spending

270
00:08:44,211 --> 00:08:46,410
plan that gives every dollar a job,

271
00:08:46,410 --> 00:08:48,797
including the job of making you happy.

272
00:08:48,977 --> 00:08:51,836
People who stick with budgets usually describe

273
00:08:51,836 --> 00:08:53,265
them as giving freedom,

274
00:08:53,265 --> 00:08:54,446
not taking it away.

275
00:08:54,626 --> 00:08:56,122
They spend without guilt,

276
00:08:56,122 --> 00:08:58,637
because they know the important things are

277
00:08:58,637 --> 00:08:59,595
already covered.

278
00:08:59,944 --> 00:09:01,463
And think in systems,

279
00:09:01,463 --> 00:09:02,475
not willpower.

280
00:09:02,655 --> 00:09:04,882
A good system makes the right choice

281
00:09:04,882 --> 00:09:05,871
the easy choice.

282
00:09:06,051 --> 00:09:07,711
Willpower runs out.

283
00:09:07,891 --> 00:09:09,412
Systems keep running.

284
00:09:09,762 --> 00:09:10,691
Angle three.

285
00:09:10,871 --> 00:09:11,626
The playbook.

286
00:09:11,806 --> 00:09:14,338
Twelve steps to build a budget system

287
00:09:14,338 --> 00:09:15,707
that actually works.

288
00:09:15,887 --> 00:09:17,282
You can set most of this up

289
00:09:17,282 --> 00:09:18,058
in one weekend.

290
00:09:18,407 --> 00:09:19,150
Step one.

291
00:09:19,330 --> 00:09:21,757
Look back at ninety days of spending.

292
00:09:22,106 --> 00:09:24,652
Download three months of bank and credit

293
00:09:24,652 --> 00:09:25,671
card statements.

294
00:09:25,851 --> 00:09:29,905
Sort every transaction into simple categories:

295
00:09:29,905 --> 00:09:32,549
housing, transportation, food,

296
00:09:32,549 --> 00:09:34,929
utilities, insurance, debt,

297
00:09:34,929 --> 00:09:37,485
subscriptions, shopping, fun,

298
00:09:37,485 --> 00:09:38,366
and other.

299
00:09:38,546 --> 00:09:39,475
Don't judge.

300
00:09:39,655 --> 00:09:40,502
Just sort.

301
00:09:40,852 --> 00:09:42,838
Ninety days gives you a much more

302
00:09:42,838 --> 00:09:44,823
realistic average than one month.

303
00:09:45,003 --> 00:09:47,203
Most people find at least one category

304
00:09:47,203 --> 00:09:49,287
that's far higher than they thought.

305
00:09:49,467 --> 00:09:50,860
That's your first opportunity.

306
00:09:51,210 --> 00:09:53,091
A tip that helps many people.

307
00:09:53,271 --> 00:09:54,118
As you sort,

308
00:09:54,118 --> 00:09:57,013
mark each transaction with a simple face:

309
00:09:57,013 --> 00:09:59,413
happy if you're glad you spent it,

310
00:09:59,413 --> 00:10:02,025
neutral, or unhappy if you regret it.

311
00:10:02,205 --> 00:10:04,557
The unhappy ones are your easiest cuts,

312
00:10:04,557 --> 00:10:06,245
because you won't miss them.

313
00:10:06,425 --> 00:10:07,995
The happy ones are what your fun

314
00:10:07,995 --> 00:10:09,026
money should protect.

315
00:10:09,376 --> 00:10:10,468
Step two.

316
00:10:10,648 --> 00:10:12,668
Calculate your true monthly costs.

317
00:10:13,017 --> 00:10:16,184
List every irregular expense you can think

318
00:10:16,184 --> 00:10:17,617
of for a full year:

319
00:10:17,617 --> 00:10:20,407
car registration, insurance premiums,

320
00:10:20,407 --> 00:10:23,573
holidays, birthdays, annual subscriptions,

321
00:10:23,573 --> 00:10:25,911
medical costs, car maintenance,

322
00:10:25,911 --> 00:10:26,891
home repairs.

323
00:10:27,071 --> 00:10:28,415
Estimate each one,

324
00:10:28,415 --> 00:10:29,311
add them up,

325
00:10:29,311 --> 00:10:30,879
and divide by twelve.

326
00:10:31,229 --> 00:10:33,500
If your irregular costs total thirty six

327
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:34,806
hundred dollars a year,

328
00:10:34,806 --> 00:10:36,907
that's three hundred dollars a month.

329
00:10:37,087 --> 00:10:39,350
Add that to your regular monthly bills,

330
00:10:39,350 --> 00:10:41,440
and you have your true monthly cost.

331
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:44,325
This one number fixes most broken budgets.

332
00:10:44,675 --> 00:10:47,849
Don't forget costs that come every two,

333
00:10:47,849 --> 00:10:49,477
three or five years,

334
00:10:49,477 --> 00:10:50,697
like new tires,

335
00:10:50,697 --> 00:10:53,220
a laptop, or a major appliance.

336
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:55,447
Divide those by the number of months

337
00:10:55,447 --> 00:10:56,697
until you expect them.

338
00:10:56,877 --> 00:10:59,312
A one thousand two hundred dollar laptop

339
00:10:59,312 --> 00:11:01,685
every four years is twenty five dollars

340
00:11:01,685 --> 00:11:02,172
a month.

341
00:11:02,352 --> 00:11:05,577
Small monthly amounts make big future costs

342
00:11:05,577 --> 00:11:06,253
painless.

343
00:11:06,602 --> 00:11:07,508
Step three.

344
00:11:07,688 --> 00:11:09,069
Choose your framework.

345
00:11:09,419 --> 00:11:11,277
Pick a simple starting structure.

346
00:11:11,457 --> 00:11:13,674
Fifty thirty twenty is a good default.

347
00:11:13,854 --> 00:11:16,341
If you're catching up or paying off

348
00:11:16,341 --> 00:11:18,473
debt, try sixty twenty twenty:

349
00:11:18,473 --> 00:11:19,609
sixty for needs,

350
00:11:19,609 --> 00:11:20,817
twenty for wants,

351
00:11:20,817 --> 00:11:22,167
twenty for savings.

352
00:11:22,347 --> 00:11:24,433
Or seventy twenty ten if your housing

353
00:11:24,433 --> 00:11:25,560
costs are very high,

354
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:27,026
with a plan to improve it.

355
00:11:27,376 --> 00:11:30,217
Some people prefer zero based budgeting,

356
00:11:30,217 --> 00:11:33,058
where every dollar of income is assigned

357
00:11:33,058 --> 00:11:35,828
a job until nothing is left unassigned.

358
00:11:36,008 --> 00:11:37,122
It takes more effort,

359
00:11:37,122 --> 00:11:38,713
but it gives you full control.

360
00:11:38,893 --> 00:11:41,308
Choose whatever you'll actually stick with.

361
00:11:41,658 --> 00:11:43,378
Whatever framework you choose,

362
00:11:43,378 --> 00:11:45,727
write down your three percentages and put

363
00:11:45,727 --> 00:11:47,045
them somewhere visible.

364
00:11:47,225 --> 00:11:48,878
When a new expense comes up,

365
00:11:48,878 --> 00:11:50,648
you can ask a simple question:

366
00:11:50,648 --> 00:11:52,595
which bucket does this come from,

367
00:11:52,595 --> 00:11:53,657
and is there room?

368
00:11:53,837 --> 00:11:57,181
That question alone prevents many impulse decisions.

369
00:11:57,531 --> 00:11:58,506
Step four.

370
00:11:58,686 --> 00:12:00,022
Set up four accounts.

371
00:12:00,371 --> 00:12:02,101
Here's the core of the system.

372
00:12:02,281 --> 00:12:03,690
Account one: Bills,

373
00:12:03,690 --> 00:12:06,804
a checking account for fixed expenses like

374
00:12:06,804 --> 00:12:10,141
rent, utilities, insurance and loan payments.

375
00:12:10,321 --> 00:12:11,770
Account two: Spending,

376
00:12:11,770 --> 00:12:14,337
a separate checking account or card for

377
00:12:14,337 --> 00:12:15,720
day to day purchases.

378
00:12:15,900 --> 00:12:17,552
Account three: Savings,

379
00:12:17,552 --> 00:12:20,498
a high yield savings account with buckets

380
00:12:20,498 --> 00:12:23,516
for your emergency fund and sinking funds.

381
00:12:23,696 --> 00:12:25,447
Account four: Investing,

382
00:12:25,447 --> 00:12:28,293
your retirement and brokerage accounts.

383
00:12:28,643 --> 00:12:31,157
Many banks and credit unions offer free

384
00:12:31,157 --> 00:12:33,090
checking and savings accounts,

385
00:12:33,090 --> 00:12:35,669
and many let you create multiple savings

386
00:12:35,669 --> 00:12:37,409
buckets inside one account.

387
00:12:37,589 --> 00:12:40,127
Setting this up usually takes an hour

388
00:12:40,127 --> 00:12:40,607
or two.

389
00:12:40,957 --> 00:12:43,210
Why separate accounts instead of one big

390
00:12:43,210 --> 00:12:44,731
account with a spreadsheet?

391
00:12:44,911 --> 00:12:47,674
Because separation makes the rules physical.

392
00:12:47,854 --> 00:12:49,643
When your bills money lives in a

393
00:12:49,643 --> 00:12:52,438
different account, you can't accidentally spend it

394
00:12:52,438 --> 00:12:52,997
on dinner.

395
00:12:53,177 --> 00:12:55,391
Your spending balance is the truth,

396
00:12:55,391 --> 00:12:56,150
at a glance,

397
00:12:56,150 --> 00:12:57,542
with no math required.

398
00:12:57,892 --> 00:12:58,868
Step five.

399
00:12:59,048 --> 00:13:00,476
Automate the payday flow.

400
00:13:00,825 --> 00:13:02,332
When your paycheck lands,

401
00:13:02,332 --> 00:13:05,226
automatic transfers move money where it belongs.

402
00:13:05,406 --> 00:13:08,482
Retirement contributions come out before the paycheck.

403
00:13:08,662 --> 00:13:10,943
Then a set amount goes to Bills,

404
00:13:10,943 --> 00:13:12,654
a set amount to Savings,

405
00:13:12,654 --> 00:13:14,792
and a set amount to Investing.

406
00:13:14,972 --> 00:13:16,644
What's left goes to Spending.

407
00:13:16,994 --> 00:13:19,047
Set all your fixed bills to autopay

408
00:13:19,047 --> 00:13:20,396
from the Bills account.

409
00:13:20,576 --> 00:13:21,623
Now your bills,

410
00:13:21,623 --> 00:13:24,766
savings and investing happen without a single

411
00:13:24,766 --> 00:13:25,394
decision.

412
00:13:25,574 --> 00:13:27,329
The only thing you manage is your

413
00:13:27,329 --> 00:13:28,233
spending account.

414
00:13:28,582 --> 00:13:30,712
Time the transfers for the day after

415
00:13:30,712 --> 00:13:32,901
payday, so the money is always there.

416
00:13:33,081 --> 00:13:35,154
And if you're paid twice a month,

417
00:13:35,154 --> 00:13:36,912
split each transfer in half.

418
00:13:37,092 --> 00:13:38,536
Within a month or two,

419
00:13:38,536 --> 00:13:41,161
you'll stop thinking about bills at all,

420
00:13:41,161 --> 00:13:42,932
which is exactly the point.

421
00:13:43,282 --> 00:13:44,501
Step six.

422
00:13:44,681 --> 00:13:46,446
Use a weekly spending number.

423
00:13:46,796 --> 00:13:49,497
Take your monthly spending money and divide

424
00:13:49,497 --> 00:13:50,941
it into weekly amounts.

425
00:13:51,121 --> 00:13:53,178
If you have one thousand dollars a

426
00:13:53,178 --> 00:13:54,387
month for groceries,

427
00:13:54,387 --> 00:13:55,839
gas, eating out and fun,

428
00:13:55,839 --> 00:13:58,319
that's about two hundred thirty dollars a

429
00:13:58,319 --> 00:13:58,621
week.

430
00:13:58,801 --> 00:14:00,403
That's your only number to watch.

431
00:14:00,753 --> 00:14:03,468
Some people transfer the weekly amount every

432
00:14:03,468 --> 00:14:03,900
Monday.

433
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:05,065
When it's gone,

434
00:14:05,065 --> 00:14:06,773
they wait until next week.

435
00:14:06,953 --> 00:14:08,241
It's simple, flexible,

436
00:14:08,241 --> 00:14:09,821
and you can see at a glance

437
00:14:09,821 --> 00:14:11,226
whether you're on track.

438
00:14:11,575 --> 00:14:13,433
Why weekly instead of monthly?

439
00:14:13,613 --> 00:14:15,252
Because a month is long,

440
00:14:15,252 --> 00:14:17,505
and it's easy to overspend in the

441
00:14:17,505 --> 00:14:19,895
first two weeks and struggle in the

442
00:14:19,895 --> 00:14:20,509
last two.

443
00:14:20,689 --> 00:14:22,442
A weekly number gives you a fresh

444
00:14:22,442 --> 00:14:23,451
start every Monday,

445
00:14:23,451 --> 00:14:25,257
and it's small enough that you can

446
00:14:25,257 --> 00:14:25,682
feel it.

447
00:14:26,031 --> 00:14:27,053
Step seven.

448
00:14:27,233 --> 00:14:28,766
Build sinking funds.

449
00:14:29,115 --> 00:14:31,386
Inside your Savings account,

450
00:14:31,386 --> 00:14:34,791
create buckets for each irregular expense:

451
00:14:34,791 --> 00:14:36,575
car repairs, holidays,

452
00:14:36,575 --> 00:14:38,683
insurance, medical, gifts,

453
00:14:38,683 --> 00:14:39,251
travel.

454
00:14:39,431 --> 00:14:41,460
Each month, a fixed amount goes to

455
00:14:41,460 --> 00:14:42,833
each one automatically.

456
00:14:43,013 --> 00:14:44,188
When the bill comes,

457
00:14:44,188 --> 00:14:45,892
the money is already waiting.

458
00:14:46,241 --> 00:14:48,568
This is the step that turns budget

459
00:14:48,568 --> 00:14:51,373
breaking surprises into planned expenses.

460
00:14:51,553 --> 00:14:54,248
A seven hundred dollar car repair becomes

461
00:14:54,248 --> 00:14:56,023
a withdrawal, not a crisis.

462
00:14:56,372 --> 00:14:59,588
Start with your three biggest irregular costs,

463
00:14:59,588 --> 00:15:01,127
often car maintenance,

464
00:15:01,127 --> 00:15:02,735
holidays and insurance.

465
00:15:02,915 --> 00:15:04,435
Add more buckets as you go.

466
00:15:04,615 --> 00:15:06,574
Many people say sinking funds are the

467
00:15:06,574 --> 00:15:08,902
single change that made their budget finally

468
00:15:08,902 --> 00:15:09,167
work.

469
00:15:09,516 --> 00:15:10,294
Step eight.

470
00:15:10,474 --> 00:15:12,111
Build your emergency fund.

471
00:15:12,461 --> 00:15:15,619
Sinking funds cover expected irregular costs.

472
00:15:15,799 --> 00:15:19,595
The emergency fund covers the truly unexpected:

473
00:15:19,595 --> 00:15:20,484
a job loss,

474
00:15:20,484 --> 00:15:22,099
a medical emergency,

475
00:15:22,099 --> 00:15:23,311
a major repair.

476
00:15:23,491 --> 00:15:25,504
Start with one thousand dollars,

477
00:15:25,504 --> 00:15:27,831
then build toward three to six months

478
00:15:27,831 --> 00:15:29,215
of essential expenses.

479
00:15:29,564 --> 00:15:31,699
Keep it in the Savings account,

480
00:15:31,699 --> 00:15:33,971
separate from your sinking funds,

481
00:15:33,971 --> 00:15:36,519
and only use it for real emergencies.

482
00:15:36,699 --> 00:15:37,755
Then refill it.

483
00:15:38,105 --> 00:15:40,450
Give the emergency fund some distance.

484
00:15:40,630 --> 00:15:42,687
Some people keep it at a separate

485
00:15:42,687 --> 00:15:44,806
bank from their everyday accounts,

486
00:15:44,806 --> 00:15:46,863
so it's available within a day or

487
00:15:46,863 --> 00:15:48,421
two but not one tap away.

488
00:15:48,601 --> 00:15:50,829
That small bit of friction protects it

489
00:15:50,829 --> 00:15:52,061
from non emergencies.

490
00:15:52,410 --> 00:15:53,304
Step nine.

491
00:15:53,484 --> 00:15:55,179
Do a ten minute weekly check.

492
00:15:55,529 --> 00:15:56,239
Once a week,

493
00:15:56,239 --> 00:15:58,665
look at your spending account balance and

494
00:15:58,665 --> 00:15:59,848
recent transactions.

495
00:16:00,028 --> 00:16:01,027
Are you on track?

496
00:16:01,207 --> 00:16:02,321
Any surprises?

497
00:16:02,501 --> 00:16:04,185
Any subscriptions you forgot?

498
00:16:04,365 --> 00:16:05,642
Ten minutes is enough.

499
00:16:05,991 --> 00:16:08,556
This small habit catches problems early,

500
00:16:08,556 --> 00:16:10,159
when they're easy to fix.

501
00:16:10,339 --> 00:16:12,988
Many people pair it with something pleasant,

502
00:16:12,988 --> 00:16:14,252
like a Sunday coffee.

503
00:16:14,601 --> 00:16:15,821
During the check,

504
00:16:15,821 --> 00:16:18,046
also look for anything unusual:

505
00:16:18,046 --> 00:16:20,128
a charge you don't recognize,

506
00:16:20,128 --> 00:16:22,640
a price increase on a subscription,

507
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:23,286
or a fee.

508
00:16:23,466 --> 00:16:25,700
Catching these early can save money and

509
00:16:25,700 --> 00:16:27,018
protect you from fraud.

510
00:16:27,368 --> 00:16:28,088
Step ten.

511
00:16:28,268 --> 00:16:30,044
Hold a monthly money meeting.

512
00:16:30,394 --> 00:16:31,247
Once a month,

513
00:16:31,247 --> 00:16:32,821
look at the big picture.

514
00:16:33,001 --> 00:16:34,731
Did your automatic transfers run?

515
00:16:34,911 --> 00:16:36,548
Are your sinking funds growing?

516
00:16:36,728 --> 00:16:38,121
How's your emergency fund?

517
00:16:38,301 --> 00:16:40,303
Is there anything coming next month,

518
00:16:40,303 --> 00:16:41,749
like a birthday or a trip?

519
00:16:42,099 --> 00:16:43,370
If you have a partner,

520
00:16:43,370 --> 00:16:44,352
do this together.

521
00:16:44,532 --> 00:16:45,630
Keep it short,

522
00:16:45,630 --> 00:16:47,984
positive and focused on goals,

523
00:16:47,984 --> 00:16:48,769
not blame.

524
00:16:48,949 --> 00:16:51,251
Many couples end it with something fun,

525
00:16:51,251 --> 00:16:53,908
like ordering their favorite takeout from the

526
00:16:53,908 --> 00:16:54,557
fun budget.

527
00:16:54,907 --> 00:16:57,229
Use the meeting to celebrate progress too.

528
00:16:57,409 --> 00:17:00,305
A sinking fund that covered a repair,

529
00:17:00,305 --> 00:17:01,557
a debt paid off,

530
00:17:01,557 --> 00:17:03,748
an emergency fund milestone.

531
00:17:03,928 --> 00:17:06,529
Money conversations don't have to be stressful.

532
00:17:06,709 --> 00:17:08,088
They can be the moment you see

533
00:17:08,088 --> 00:17:08,915
your plan working.

534
00:17:09,264 --> 00:17:10,321
Step eleven.

535
00:17:10,501 --> 00:17:12,625
Handle overspending without guilt.

536
00:17:12,975 --> 00:17:14,891
You will overspend sometimes.

537
00:17:15,071 --> 00:17:16,174
Everyone does.

538
00:17:16,354 --> 00:17:17,444
When it happens,

539
00:17:17,444 --> 00:17:19,691
move money from another category,

540
00:17:19,691 --> 00:17:22,007
reduce next week's spending a bit,

541
00:17:22,007 --> 00:17:24,186
or adjust the category if it was

542
00:17:24,186 --> 00:17:25,003
unrealistic.

543
00:17:25,353 --> 00:17:27,220
What you don't do is abandon the

544
00:17:27,220 --> 00:17:27,629
system.

545
00:17:27,809 --> 00:17:30,015
One bad week doesn't ruin a budget.

546
00:17:30,195 --> 00:17:31,193
Quitting does.

547
00:17:31,543 --> 00:17:34,301
If one category goes over every single

548
00:17:34,301 --> 00:17:37,058
month, that's not a willpower problem.

549
00:17:37,238 --> 00:17:38,411
It's a planning problem.

550
00:17:38,591 --> 00:17:41,354
Raise that category and lower another one.

551
00:17:41,534 --> 00:17:43,904
The budget should fit your real life,

552
00:17:43,904 --> 00:17:45,249
not an imaginary one.

553
00:17:45,599 --> 00:17:46,528
Step twelve.

554
00:17:46,708 --> 00:17:48,125
Do an annual reset.

555
00:17:48,474 --> 00:17:49,227
Once a year,

556
00:17:49,227 --> 00:17:50,355
review everything.

557
00:17:50,535 --> 00:17:52,846
Update your irregular expenses list.

558
00:17:53,026 --> 00:17:55,768
Raise your savings and investing transfers,

559
00:17:55,768 --> 00:17:57,298
especially after raises.

560
00:17:57,478 --> 00:17:59,695
Cancel subscriptions you don't use.

561
00:17:59,875 --> 00:18:01,801
Shop around for insurance,

562
00:18:01,801 --> 00:18:03,208
phone and internet.

563
00:18:03,557 --> 00:18:06,050
This yearly reset often finds hundreds or

564
00:18:06,050 --> 00:18:08,300
even thousands of dollars in savings,

565
00:18:08,300 --> 00:18:10,672
and keeps your budget aligned with your

566
00:18:10,672 --> 00:18:10,976
life.

567
00:18:11,326 --> 00:18:13,363
A good time for the reset is

568
00:18:13,363 --> 00:18:16,346
early January, or right after your yearly

569
00:18:16,346 --> 00:18:16,783
raise.

570
00:18:16,963 --> 00:18:17,619
Pick a date,

571
00:18:17,619 --> 00:18:18,932
put it on your calendar,

572
00:18:18,932 --> 00:18:20,736
and treat it like an appointment.

573
00:18:20,916 --> 00:18:22,676
One afternoon a year can keep the

574
00:18:22,676 --> 00:18:23,795
whole system healthy.

575
00:18:24,145 --> 00:18:25,446
Budgeting isn't new.

576
00:18:25,626 --> 00:18:28,767
For generations, many families used the envelope

577
00:18:28,767 --> 00:18:29,225
system.

578
00:18:29,405 --> 00:18:33,246
On payday, cash went into labeled envelopes:

579
00:18:33,246 --> 00:18:35,167
rent, groceries, coal,

580
00:18:35,167 --> 00:18:35,953
clothing.

581
00:18:36,133 --> 00:18:38,055
When an envelope was empty,

582
00:18:38,055 --> 00:18:40,475
spending in that category stopped.

583
00:18:40,655 --> 00:18:41,547
It was simple,

584
00:18:41,547 --> 00:18:44,416
visual, and it worked without any technology.

585
00:18:44,766 --> 00:18:47,736
In 1970, a manager at Texas Instruments

586
00:18:47,736 --> 00:18:51,391
named Peter Pyhrr described zero based budgeting

587
00:18:51,391 --> 00:18:52,533
for businesses.

588
00:18:52,713 --> 00:18:55,532
Instead of starting from last year's budget,

589
00:18:55,532 --> 00:18:57,966
every expense had to be justified from

590
00:18:57,966 --> 00:18:58,286
zero.

591
00:18:58,466 --> 00:19:01,370
The idea later spread to personal finance:

592
00:19:01,370 --> 00:19:03,029
every dollar gets a job.

593
00:19:03,379 --> 00:19:06,449
In 2005, Elizabeth Warren and Amelia Warren

594
00:19:06,449 --> 00:19:09,733
Tyagi popularized the fifty thirty twenty rule

595
00:19:09,733 --> 00:19:11,518
as a simpler alternative.

596
00:19:11,698 --> 00:19:13,783
And in the 2000s and 2010s,

597
00:19:13,783 --> 00:19:17,335
budgeting apps began linking bank accounts and

598
00:19:17,335 --> 00:19:20,115
categorizing spending automatically.

599
00:19:20,465 --> 00:19:22,759
The four account system in this video

600
00:19:22,759 --> 00:19:24,308
borrows from all of them.

601
00:19:24,488 --> 00:19:27,263
The envelope idea of separate buckets.

602
00:19:27,443 --> 00:19:29,966
The zero based idea of giving every

603
00:19:29,966 --> 00:19:30,903
dollar a job.

604
00:19:31,083 --> 00:19:33,718
The simplicity of fifty thirty twenty.

605
00:19:33,898 --> 00:19:35,284
And modern automation,

606
00:19:35,284 --> 00:19:37,300
so it runs without daily effort.

607
00:19:37,650 --> 00:19:38,672
Quick pause.

608
00:19:38,852 --> 00:19:40,733
If this system is helping you,

609
00:19:40,733 --> 00:19:42,300
subscribe to the channel.

610
00:19:42,480 --> 00:19:44,261
It's free, and it helps me keep

611
00:19:44,261 --> 00:19:45,870
making these videos for you.

612
00:19:46,050 --> 00:19:47,815
Now, the sample budgets.

613
00:19:48,165 --> 00:19:50,586
Let's see the system at three income

614
00:19:50,586 --> 00:19:51,056
levels.

615
00:19:51,236 --> 00:19:53,817
First, three thousand dollars a month of

616
00:19:53,817 --> 00:19:54,720
take home pay,

617
00:19:54,720 --> 00:19:55,752
a single person.

618
00:19:55,932 --> 00:19:58,423
Bills account: fourteen hundred,

619
00:19:58,423 --> 00:20:00,836
including rent with a roommate,

620
00:20:00,836 --> 00:20:04,261
utilities, phone, insurance and a small loan

621
00:20:04,261 --> 00:20:04,884
payment.

622
00:20:05,064 --> 00:20:07,827
Savings: two hundred fifty for sinking funds

623
00:20:07,827 --> 00:20:10,276
and one hundred fifty for the emergency

624
00:20:10,276 --> 00:20:10,590
fund.

625
00:20:10,940 --> 00:20:14,018
Investing: one hundred fifty to a Roth

626
00:20:14,018 --> 00:20:17,581
IRA, plus a workplace match through payroll.

627
00:20:17,761 --> 00:20:20,372
Spending: about one thousand fifty,

628
00:20:20,372 --> 00:20:22,983
or roughly two hundred forty a week

629
00:20:22,983 --> 00:20:24,400
for groceries, gas,

630
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:26,190
fun and everything else.

631
00:20:26,370 --> 00:20:28,492
It's tight, but every dollar has a

632
00:20:28,492 --> 00:20:30,364
job, and the future is funded.

633
00:20:30,713 --> 00:20:33,632
Second, five thousand dollars a month,

634
00:20:33,632 --> 00:20:35,322
a couple with no kids.

635
00:20:35,502 --> 00:20:37,499
Bills: twenty three hundred,

636
00:20:37,499 --> 00:20:39,354
including rent, utilities,

637
00:20:39,354 --> 00:20:41,493
insurance and one car payment.

638
00:20:41,673 --> 00:20:44,444
Savings: four hundred for sinking funds and

639
00:20:44,444 --> 00:20:46,828
three hundred for the emergency fund.

640
00:20:47,008 --> 00:20:49,627
Investing: six hundred in IRAs,

641
00:20:49,627 --> 00:20:52,836
plus retirement plans through payroll.

642
00:20:53,186 --> 00:20:55,651
Spending: about fourteen hundred,

643
00:20:55,651 --> 00:20:58,490
around three hundred twenty a week for

644
00:20:58,490 --> 00:21:01,104
groceries, gas, dining out and fun.

645
00:21:01,284 --> 00:21:04,137
That's about eighteen percent going to the

646
00:21:04,137 --> 00:21:07,058
emergency fund and investing from take home

647
00:21:07,058 --> 00:21:09,911
pay alone, plus the payroll contributions.

648
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:12,948
Third, eight thousand dollars a month,

649
00:21:12,948 --> 00:21:14,150
a family of four.

650
00:21:14,330 --> 00:21:16,282
Bills: thirty nine hundred,

651
00:21:16,282 --> 00:21:18,452
including mortgage, utilities,

652
00:21:18,452 --> 00:21:21,272
insurance, childcare and a car payment.

653
00:21:21,452 --> 00:21:24,134
Savings: six hundred for sinking funds and

654
00:21:24,134 --> 00:21:26,433
four hundred for the emergency fund.

655
00:21:26,613 --> 00:21:28,817
Investing: one thousand,

656
00:21:28,817 --> 00:21:32,583
plus a small 529 contribution and payroll

657
00:21:32,583 --> 00:21:34,879
retirement contributions.

658
00:21:35,229 --> 00:21:37,456
Spending: about two thousand,

659
00:21:37,456 --> 00:21:40,220
around four hundred sixty a week for

660
00:21:40,220 --> 00:21:43,368
groceries, gas, kids' activities and fun.

661
00:21:43,548 --> 00:21:45,034
Same four accounts.

662
00:21:45,214 --> 00:21:46,654
Same automatic flow.

663
00:21:46,834 --> 00:21:47,902
Same weekly number.

664
00:21:48,082 --> 00:21:49,231
The amounts change.

665
00:21:49,411 --> 00:21:50,468
The system doesn't.

666
00:21:50,817 --> 00:21:52,142
Same four accounts,

667
00:21:52,142 --> 00:21:53,328
same payday flow,

668
00:21:53,328 --> 00:21:55,281
same weekly spending number,

669
00:21:55,281 --> 00:21:57,931
whether you bring home three thousand,

670
00:21:57,931 --> 00:22:00,721
five thousand or eight thousand a month.

671
00:22:01,070 --> 00:22:02,522
Let's compare two people.

672
00:22:02,702 --> 00:22:05,438
Nina downloads a detailed budget app,

673
00:22:05,438 --> 00:22:07,138
sets thirty categories,

674
00:22:07,138 --> 00:22:08,913
and tracks every coffee.

675
00:22:09,093 --> 00:22:10,160
For two weeks,

676
00:22:10,160 --> 00:22:11,380
she's motivated.

677
00:22:11,560 --> 00:22:13,116
Then a busy month hits.

678
00:22:13,296 --> 00:22:14,538
She stops logging.

679
00:22:14,718 --> 00:22:16,878
Her categories fall out of date.

680
00:22:17,058 --> 00:22:18,129
By month two,

681
00:22:18,129 --> 00:22:21,179
she feels guilty and deletes the app.

682
00:22:21,529 --> 00:22:24,092
Tom sets up four accounts and automates

683
00:22:24,092 --> 00:22:24,815
everything.

684
00:22:24,995 --> 00:22:27,202
His only rule is a weekly spending

685
00:22:27,202 --> 00:22:29,604
number of two hundred eighty dollars.

686
00:22:29,784 --> 00:22:31,287
Some weeks he goes over,

687
00:22:31,287 --> 00:22:32,978
so the next week he eats at

688
00:22:32,978 --> 00:22:33,604
home more.

689
00:22:33,784 --> 00:22:36,175
He spends ten minutes on Sunday checking.

690
00:22:36,355 --> 00:22:37,147
A year later,

691
00:22:37,147 --> 00:22:38,365
he's still doing it,

692
00:22:38,365 --> 00:22:40,009
his emergency fund is full,

693
00:22:40,009 --> 00:22:42,079
and he's investing more than ever.

694
00:22:42,429 --> 00:22:45,146
Nina didn't fail because she lacked discipline.

695
00:22:45,326 --> 00:22:47,282
She chose a system that needed too

696
00:22:47,282 --> 00:22:47,915
much of it.

697
00:22:48,095 --> 00:22:50,303
When she switches to the four account

698
00:22:50,303 --> 00:22:52,333
approach, she finds it far easier.

699
00:22:52,513 --> 00:22:54,766
The best budget isn't the most detailed

700
00:22:54,766 --> 00:22:54,997
one.

701
00:22:55,177 --> 00:22:56,687
It's the one you'll keep using.

702
00:22:57,036 --> 00:22:59,219
Here's the lesson from Nina and Tom.

703
00:22:59,399 --> 00:23:00,959
Detail feels productive,

704
00:23:00,959 --> 00:23:02,975
but it often isn't sustainable.

705
00:23:03,155 --> 00:23:04,726
If you love tracking,

706
00:23:04,726 --> 00:23:05,175
track.

707
00:23:05,355 --> 00:23:06,018
If you don't,

708
00:23:06,018 --> 00:23:07,956
let the account structure do the work.

709
00:23:08,136 --> 00:23:10,332
Either way, the goal is the same:

710
00:23:10,332 --> 00:23:12,261
bills covered, future funded,

711
00:23:12,261 --> 00:23:14,923
and spending money you can enjoy without

712
00:23:14,923 --> 00:23:15,322
worry.

713
00:23:15,502 --> 00:23:17,683
Choose the version you'll still be using

714
00:23:17,683 --> 00:23:18,556
a year from now.

715
00:23:18,905 --> 00:23:21,792
What if your income changes every month,

716
00:23:21,792 --> 00:23:23,957
like freelancers, gig workers,

717
00:23:23,957 --> 00:23:25,833
salespeople on commission,

718
00:23:25,833 --> 00:23:27,276
or seasonal workers?

719
00:23:27,456 --> 00:23:29,188
The system still works,

720
00:23:29,188 --> 00:23:30,393
with one change.

721
00:23:30,573 --> 00:23:31,860
Add a fifth account:

722
00:23:31,860 --> 00:23:32,826
a holding tank.

723
00:23:33,175 --> 00:23:35,613
All income goes into the holding tank

724
00:23:35,613 --> 00:23:36,008
first.

725
00:23:36,188 --> 00:23:38,433
Then, on the same day each month,

726
00:23:38,433 --> 00:23:40,881
you pay yourself a fixed salary from

727
00:23:40,881 --> 00:23:43,534
it, based on a conservative estimate of

728
00:23:43,534 --> 00:23:44,350
your income.

729
00:23:44,530 --> 00:23:47,222
That salary flows into the four accounts

730
00:23:47,222 --> 00:23:47,827
as usual.

731
00:23:48,007 --> 00:23:49,174
In good months,

732
00:23:49,174 --> 00:23:50,341
the tank grows.

733
00:23:50,521 --> 00:23:51,513
In slow months,

734
00:23:51,513 --> 00:23:53,365
it keeps your salary steady.

735
00:23:53,715 --> 00:23:55,721
Aim to build the holding tank to

736
00:23:55,721 --> 00:23:57,538
at least one or two months of

737
00:23:57,538 --> 00:23:59,857
your salary, on top of your emergency

738
00:23:59,857 --> 00:24:00,170
fund.

739
00:24:00,350 --> 00:24:02,363
And if you're self employed,

740
00:24:02,363 --> 00:24:04,520
set aside money for taxes in a

741
00:24:04,520 --> 00:24:07,251
separate bucket, since you may need to

742
00:24:07,251 --> 00:24:10,486
make quarterly estimated payments to the IRS.

743
00:24:10,836 --> 00:24:12,880
Couples have extra challenges:

744
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:14,651
different spending styles,

745
00:24:14,651 --> 00:24:17,988
different incomes, and sometimes different goals.

746
00:24:18,168 --> 00:24:20,485
A common approach is yours,

747
00:24:20,485 --> 00:24:21,686
mine and ours.

748
00:24:21,866 --> 00:24:25,149
Shared bills, savings and investing come from

749
00:24:25,149 --> 00:24:26,243
a joint system.

750
00:24:26,423 --> 00:24:29,277
Each person also gets personal spending money

751
00:24:29,277 --> 00:24:30,800
with no questions asked.

752
00:24:31,149 --> 00:24:33,841
How much each person contributes can be

753
00:24:33,841 --> 00:24:36,118
equal, or proportional to income.

754
00:24:36,298 --> 00:24:38,121
There's no single right answer.

755
00:24:38,301 --> 00:24:40,235
What matters is that it feels fair

756
00:24:40,235 --> 00:24:41,088
to both of you.

757
00:24:41,268 --> 00:24:44,240
The monthly money meeting keeps everything transparent.

758
00:24:44,589 --> 00:24:46,075
Which tool should you use?

759
00:24:46,255 --> 00:24:48,159
Honestly, any of them can work.

760
00:24:48,339 --> 00:24:50,877
Budgeting apps can link your accounts and

761
00:24:50,877 --> 00:24:52,981
categorize spending automatically,

762
00:24:52,981 --> 00:24:54,528
though some charge a fee.

763
00:24:54,708 --> 00:24:57,199
A simple spreadsheet is free and fully

764
00:24:57,199 --> 00:24:58,051
customizable.

765
00:24:58,231 --> 00:25:01,111
And cash envelopes work well for people

766
00:25:01,111 --> 00:25:02,956
who overspend with cards.

767
00:25:03,306 --> 00:25:05,689
The four account system works with all

768
00:25:05,689 --> 00:25:07,758
of them, because most of the work

769
00:25:07,758 --> 00:25:10,015
is done by the account structure and

770
00:25:10,015 --> 00:25:11,770
automation, not by tracking.

771
00:25:11,950 --> 00:25:13,668
The tool is just a window.

772
00:25:13,848 --> 00:25:15,392
The system is the engine.

773
00:25:15,742 --> 00:25:18,194
Now, if your budget doesn't balance yet,

774
00:25:18,194 --> 00:25:19,910
where do you find the money?

775
00:25:20,090 --> 00:25:23,148
Here's a quick category by category guide,

776
00:25:23,148 --> 00:25:25,477
from biggest to smallest impact.

777
00:25:25,827 --> 00:25:26,524
Housing.

778
00:25:26,704 --> 00:25:28,411
It's usually the biggest lever.

779
00:25:28,591 --> 00:25:30,620
Options include a roommate,

780
00:25:30,620 --> 00:25:33,175
a cheaper unit at your next lease,

781
00:25:33,175 --> 00:25:36,633
negotiating rent, or refinancing a mortgage if

782
00:25:36,633 --> 00:25:38,587
rates and fees make sense.

783
00:25:38,767 --> 00:25:41,126
Even a two hundred dollar monthly reduction

784
00:25:41,126 --> 00:25:42,772
is twenty four hundred a year.

785
00:25:43,122 --> 00:25:44,318
Transportation.

786
00:25:44,498 --> 00:25:45,747
Keep cars longer,

787
00:25:45,747 --> 00:25:47,289
avoid large payments,

788
00:25:47,289 --> 00:25:50,447
compare insurance quotes every year or two,

789
00:25:50,447 --> 00:25:53,752
and consider whether a household really needs

790
00:25:53,752 --> 00:25:54,413
two cars.

791
00:25:54,593 --> 00:25:55,666
For some people,

792
00:25:55,666 --> 00:25:58,280
public transit or biking for some trips

793
00:25:58,280 --> 00:26:00,224
can save a surprising amount.

794
00:26:00,574 --> 00:26:00,969
Food.

795
00:26:01,149 --> 00:26:03,081
Plan meals, cook in batches,

796
00:26:03,081 --> 00:26:04,254
shop with a list,

797
00:26:04,254 --> 00:26:05,911
and limit delivery apps,

798
00:26:05,911 --> 00:26:08,188
whose fees and markups can add up

799
00:26:08,188 --> 00:26:08,533
fast.

800
00:26:08,713 --> 00:26:10,420
You don't have to stop eating out.

801
00:26:10,600 --> 00:26:12,499
Just decide in advance how much of

802
00:26:12,499 --> 00:26:14,176
your weekly number goes to it.

803
00:26:14,525 --> 00:26:15,512
Insurance.

804
00:26:15,692 --> 00:26:18,156
Shop your auto and home or renters

805
00:26:18,156 --> 00:26:19,605
insurance regularly.

806
00:26:19,785 --> 00:26:22,592
Consider higher deductibles if you have a

807
00:26:22,592 --> 00:26:24,029
solid emergency fund,

808
00:26:24,029 --> 00:26:25,741
which can lower premiums.

809
00:26:25,921 --> 00:26:27,711
And make sure you're not paying for

810
00:26:27,711 --> 00:26:28,939
coverage you don't need.

811
00:26:29,289 --> 00:26:31,890
Phone, internet and utilities.

812
00:26:32,070 --> 00:26:34,949
Call your providers and ask about cheaper

813
00:26:34,949 --> 00:26:36,354
plans or promotions.

814
00:26:36,534 --> 00:26:39,176
Many people overpay for phone data or

815
00:26:39,176 --> 00:26:41,317
internet speed they don't use.

816
00:26:41,497 --> 00:26:44,212
Prepaid phone plans can cost much less

817
00:26:44,212 --> 00:26:46,141
than traditional contracts.

818
00:26:46,491 --> 00:26:47,594
Subscriptions.

819
00:26:47,774 --> 00:26:50,137
List every recurring charge from the last

820
00:26:50,137 --> 00:26:50,886
three months.

821
00:26:51,066 --> 00:26:52,691
Cancel what you don't use.

822
00:26:52,871 --> 00:26:56,107
Rotate streaming services instead of keeping them

823
00:26:56,107 --> 00:26:56,900
all at once.

824
00:26:57,080 --> 00:26:59,805
Remember, the average person in that survey

825
00:26:59,805 --> 00:27:03,163
underestimated their subscriptions by more than half.

826
00:27:03,513 --> 00:27:04,825
And banking fees.

827
00:27:05,005 --> 00:27:08,808
Overdraft fees, monthly maintenance fees and ATM

828
00:27:08,808 --> 00:27:10,392
fees are pure waste.

829
00:27:10,572 --> 00:27:13,017
Many banks and credit unions offer free

830
00:27:13,017 --> 00:27:14,647
accounts with no minimums.

831
00:27:14,827 --> 00:27:17,005
Switching can save hundreds of dollars a

832
00:27:17,005 --> 00:27:17,277
year.

833
00:27:17,627 --> 00:27:20,031
Mistake one: making the budget too tight.

834
00:27:20,211 --> 00:27:22,286
A budget with no fun money and

835
00:27:22,286 --> 00:27:25,122
no buffer usually collapses within weeks.

836
00:27:25,302 --> 00:27:27,255
Plan for a life you actually want

837
00:27:27,255 --> 00:27:27,728
to live.

838
00:27:28,078 --> 00:27:31,190
Mistake two: forgetting irregular expenses.

839
00:27:31,370 --> 00:27:33,608
This is the number one reason budgets

840
00:27:33,608 --> 00:27:33,970
break.

841
00:27:34,150 --> 00:27:35,683
Sinking funds solve it.

842
00:27:36,033 --> 00:27:38,715
Mistake three: too many categories.

843
00:27:38,895 --> 00:27:41,751
Thirty categories means thirty decisions.

844
00:27:41,931 --> 00:27:44,225
Most people do better with a handful

845
00:27:44,225 --> 00:27:46,900
of buckets and one weekly spending number.

846
00:27:47,250 --> 00:27:49,816
Mistake four: budgeting on gross pay.

847
00:27:49,996 --> 00:27:52,121
Your budget should be built on take

848
00:27:52,121 --> 00:27:54,854
home pay, after taxes and payroll deductions,

849
00:27:54,854 --> 00:27:57,647
because that's the money that actually reaches

850
00:27:57,647 --> 00:27:58,437
your account.

851
00:27:58,786 --> 00:27:59,988
And mistake five:

852
00:27:59,988 --> 00:28:01,259
never updating it.

853
00:28:01,439 --> 00:28:02,426
Life changes.

854
00:28:02,606 --> 00:28:04,781
Raises, moves, kids,

855
00:28:04,781 --> 00:28:05,869
new goals.

856
00:28:06,049 --> 00:28:07,593
Your budget should change too.

857
00:28:07,773 --> 00:28:09,929
The monthly meeting and the annual reset

858
00:28:09,929 --> 00:28:10,791
keep it current.

859
00:28:11,141 --> 00:28:12,756
Let's go back to Tom and see

860
00:28:12,756 --> 00:28:14,601
what one year of the system did.

861
00:28:14,781 --> 00:28:15,565
In month one,

862
